---
references:
  book:
    id: book
    type: book
    title: A Book
    authors:
      - firstName: John
        lastName: Smith
    language: en
---

The citation head is `[^reference_id "locator"]`: the caret is the slot tag, the
bare content is the reference and the quoted one the locator (§7.3). They land in
the preset's two fields, so the node carries `reference_id` and `locator`, and an
adjacent extras head decorates the node without ever writing them (§6.2):

Minim [^book "hlm. 45"]@@citeX{.paper, #smith, note: "short"} esse do ut anim proident.

The locator stays the construct value even when the extras ask for one:

[^book "hlm. 45"]@@citeX{locator: "dropped", .thin}

A locator is part of the citation identity: the same reference with a different
(or no) locator gets a new number.

[^book]@@citeX{.repeat}

A repeated `(reference_id, locator)` keeps its `citation_number`:

[^book]@@citeX{.again}

Every position the preset does not name stays a bare field, so a paren's contents
are ordinary fields of the node (§6.1, D-7):

[^book "hlm. 7"](paperback "Reprint")@@citeX

A head without a reference is an error, not a citation (§7.3): `[^]` and
`[^"book"]` stay text, and so does a citation of a reference the document does
not know. The retired `[^^](book "hlm. 45")` spelling is not a citation either —
its second caret is now the reference:

[^] and [^"book"] and [^nosuch]
